It is obvious that the correspondence between $X$ and $\uparrow X$ is inclusion *reversing*, in the sense that

$$
X \subseteq Y \quad \text{iff} \quad \uparrow Y \subseteq \uparrow X
$$

for all $X, Y \in \mathcal{D}$.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`principal_le_iff`).** For neighbourhoods $X$ and $Y$, one has $\uparrow X \le \uparrow Y$ in the approximation order if and only if $Y\subseteq X$. Equivalently, $X\subseteq Y$ if and only if $\uparrow Y\subseteq \uparrow X$ as families of neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* If $\uparrow X\le \uparrow Y$, then $X$ belongs to $\uparrow Y$, so $Y\subseteq X$. Conversely, if $Y\subseteq X$ and $Z$ belongs to $\uparrow X$, then $X\subseteq Z$, hence $Y\subseteq Z$, so $Z$ belongs to $\uparrow Y$. ∎
